            <ArticleTitle>Australian management strategies for oral opioid use in non-malignant pain.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>99-101</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Chronic non-cancer pain is a complex biopsychosocial phenomenon; as such it is unlikely that any one treatment modality will achieve relief. There is increasing availability of oral opioid preparations and an associated increased level of prescribing; consequently in 1997 the Australian Pain Society drew up some management strategies for their use. This paper reviews the principles of those strategies, stressing that oral opioids should not be used in isolation nor used to treat 'distress' often associated with chronic pain. The place of a trial of opioid and informed consent are discussed.</AbstractText>
